An environmental emergency plan must include the following:
(a) a description of the properties and characteristics of the substance and the maximum expected quantity of the substance at the facility [1];
(b) a description of the commercial, manufacturing, processing or other activity involving the substance that takes place at the facility [1];
(c) a description of the facility and surrounding area that may be affected by an environmental emergency referred to in paragraph (d), including any hospitals, schools, residential, commercial or industrial buildings and any highways, public transit infrastructure, parks, forests, wildlife habitats, water sources or water bodies [1];
(d) an identification of any environmental emergency reasonably expected to occur at the facility that would likely cause harm to the environment or constitute a danger to human life or health, including the emergency referred to in paragraph (e) and, if applicable, the emergency more likely to occur than that in paragraph (e) having the longest impact distance outside the boundary of the facility [1];
(e) an identification of the harm to the environment or danger to human life or health likely to result from an environmental emergency involving the release of (i) the maximum quantity that could be contained in the container system with the largest maximum capacity, if a quantity is in a container system, and (ii) the maximum expected quantity that will not be in a container system, if a quantity is not in a container system [1];
(f) an identification of the harm to the environment or danger to human life or health likely to result from the environmental emergency identified under paragraph (d), if any, that is more likely to occur than that in paragraph (e) and would have the longest impact distance outside the boundary of the facility [1];
(g) a description of measures to be taken to prevent and prepare for the emergencies identified under paragraph (d), and measures that will be taken to respond to and recover from them if they occur [1];
(h) a list of position titles of persons who will make decisions and take a leadership role in an environmental emergency, and a description of their roles and responsibilities [1];
(i) a list of environmental emergency training provided or to be provided to prepare personnel at the facility who will respond to an emergency identified under paragraph (d) [1];
(j) a list of necessary emergency response equipment for measures described in paragraph (g) and the equipment's location [1];
(k) a description of measures to be taken by a responsible person, or jointly with local authorities, to communicate before an emergency occurs with members of the public who may be adversely affected by the emergency referred to in paragraph (f) to inform them of (i) the possibility of occurrence, (ii) potential effects on the environment and human life or health taking into account paragraphs (a) to (c), and (iii) protection measures and means of communication if an emergency occurs [1];
(l) a description of measures to be taken by a responsible person, or jointly with local authorities, to communicate during and after a release with adversely affected members of the public to provide guidance on actions they can take to reduce harm or danger, including an explanation of how those actions help [1];
(m) the position title of the person who will communicate with the public referred to in paragraphs (k) and (l) [1];
(n) a description of consultations had with local authorities, if any, regarding the measures referred to in paragraphs (k) and (l) [1];
(o) a plan of the facility showing the location of any substances in relation to physical features of the facility [1].

Guidance adds:
- The plan must be site-specific [4].
- A single plan may cover one or more substances if it addresses the full range of potential hazards on site and the prevention, preparedness, response, and recovery elements for identified scenarios [4].

